Part 1 — Create a throwaway test CA + client cert (so you can test mTLS now)
Run these on a Linux/macOS box (or WSL) with OpenSSL:
Create a test Certificate Authority (CA)
 
 
 
 
Shell
 
openssl genrsa -out test-ca.key 4096

openssl req -x509 -new -nodes -key test-ca.key -sha256 -days 3650 -out test-ca.crt -subj "/CN=Test-CA"

Show more lines
 
Create a client key + CSR
 
 
 
 
Shell
 
openssl genrsa -out client.key 2048

openssl req -new -key client.key -out client.csr -subj "/CN=mtls-test-client"

``

Show more lines
 
Sign the client cert with your test CA
 
 
 
 
Shell
 
openssl x509 -req -in client.csr -CA test-ca.crt -CAkey test-ca.key -CAcreateserial -out client.crt -days 365 -sha256

Show more lines
 
Create a PEM that includes the client cert + key (handy for tools)
 
 
 
 
Shell
 
cat client.crt client.key > client.pem


Show more lines
 
You now have:
test-ca.crt = the CA you’ll trust in API Gateway (truststore)
client.crt + client.key (or client.pem) = what your test client presents
Part 2 — Set up an AWS API Gateway endpoint (backend can be dummy)
Option A (fastest): Lambda proxy that always returns 200
Create a Lambda function mtls-soap-test that returns a fixed response (anything).
Create API Gateway REST API
Add Resource /verify and Method POST
Integrate with the Lambda (Lambda proxy integration is fine)
Deploy to a stage, e.g. test
At this point you have an execute-api URL like:https://abc123.execute-api.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/test/verify
(You won’t use this URL for mTLS; you’ll use a custom domain.)
Part 3 — Configure mTLS on a Custom Domain in API Gateway
API Gateway mTLS only works via Custom Domain Name.
3.1 Put the truststore in S3
Create an S3 bucket (example: my-mtls-truststore-bucket)
Upload test-ca.crt to S3, e.g.:
Bucket: my-mtls-truststore-bucket
Key: truststore/test-ca.crt
3.2 Create / attach an ACM server certificate
You need a normal server TLS cert for your API custom domain, in the same region as the API.
In ACM, request a cert for your domain name (ex: ivs-test-api.yourcompany.com)
Validate it (DNS validation typically)
3.3 Create the API Gateway Custom Domain + enable mTLS
In API Gateway:
Custom domain names → Create
Domain name: ivs-test-api.yourcompany.com
Choose endpoint type (Regional is common)
Choose the ACM cert
Enable Mutual TLS authentication
Truststore:
S3 URI: point to s3://my-mtls-truststore-bucket/truststore/test-ca.crt
(and whatever versioning option it asks for)
3.4 Map the base path to your API/stage
In the custom domain, add an API mapping:
API: your REST API
Stage: test
Path: (blank) or verify—your preference
3.5 DNS
Create a DNS record so your custom domain resolves to the API Gateway domain target.
Part 4 — Create a “toy SOAP request” file
Create request.xml:
 
 
 
 
Shell
 
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<soapenv:Envelope xmlns:soapenv="http://schemas.xmlsoap.org/soap/envelope/">

  <soapenv:Body>

    <Ping xmlns="urn:mtls-test">Hello</Ping>

  </soapenv:Body>

</soapenv:Envelope>

Show more lines
 
Again: SOAP content doesn’t matter for mTLS—this is just a convenient body.
Part 5 — Run the actual mTLS tests (this is the proof)
Assume your final URL is:https://ivs-test-api.yourcompany.com/verify
Test 1: No client certificate (should FAIL at TLS)
 
 
 
 
Shell
 
curl -v https://ivs-test-api.yourcompany.com/verify \

  -H "Content-Type: text/xml" \

  --data-binary @request.xml


Show more lines
 
Expected result: TLS handshake failure (you should not get an HTTP 200/403 from your Lambda; it should fail earlier).
Test 2: Correct client certificate (should SUCCEED)
 
 
 
 
Shell
 
curl -v https://ivs-test-api.yourcompany.com/verify \

  --cert client.crt --key client.key \

  -H "Content-Type: text/xml" \

  --data-binary @request.xml

Show more lines
 
Expected: you reach API Gateway and get whatever HTTP response your backend returns (even 200 with dummy body).
Test 3: Wrong certificate (should FAIL)
Generate another CA/client quickly (or just trust a different CA) and try again; expected handshake failure.
